 17592186045417] [17592186133824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133824 #edb/ref 1039 "Factoring a Constant Out of the Norm" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133824 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133824 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "837e0fa4-b8d3-59c1-87c7-44317adafaf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1045 "The Norm of a Vector in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133982 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "79df4a45-c922-5703-9054-3312861d012f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134239 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134239 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134239 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134239 #edb/ref 1071 "If $‖3x‖=4$, find $‖\\frac{x}{5}‖.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134239 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf155ba0-24a3-52c8-ab4a-850b59e89a2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134239 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n‖kx‖=|k|\\cdot ‖x‖\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we can solve for the norm $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖3x‖ & =4 \\\\ |3|\\cdot ‖x‖ & =4 \\\\ ‖x‖ & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $‖\\frac{x}{5}‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\frac{x}{5}‖ & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134627 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134627 #edb/ref 1061 20183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4d2c70c6-8130-51bd-bba0-328eb720e63e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134628 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134628 #edb/ref 1061 20184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134628 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41dc1ce1-3949-5bd4-a737-625779c43d60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134628 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134629 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134629 #edb/ref 1061 20185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134629 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2bd1009-695c-5967-93d6-75e93251bdf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134629 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134630 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134630 #edb/ref 1061 20186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134630 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33e38aa0-acfb-5a0e-87b4-4d4fdfb38455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134630 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134631 #edb/ref 1061 20187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134631 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd0fcef8-cb65-59c0-81f7-f73c86d09b7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134631 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134735 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134735 #edb/ref 1061 23165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134735 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c4535616-82fc-59ed-962a-9ca36d7214a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134735 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134736 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134736 #edb/ref 1061 23169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134736 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bba7150c-8928-5dd6-b8e9-d9d8983db01f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134736 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134737 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134737 #edb/ref 1061 23172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134737 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b0b507b6-b42b-5f48-ba2d-8f36454611f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134737 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135031 #edb/ref 1064 "The vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ paired with the dot product, is called a **Euclidean space**. \n\nFor a vector ![](images/83/838ca0a3284985fa58d5ac894740b15f9bbbfd931299834cc8e062e364bbf983.svg) in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$  the **norm** (or **length**)  is given by \n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert = \\sqrt{\\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}} = \\sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2+\\ldots+x_n^2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector ![](images/2a/2a4e0070bdec6033ab8b71cbb77dbd8569bea8672c0e17cedbf876a77ce56bb2.svg) The norm of this vector is calculated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the dot product of a vector with itself is always non-negative, $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot\\mathbf{x}\\geq 0,$ so it's always possible to take the square root $\\sqrt{ \\mathbf{x}\\cdot\\mathbf{x} }.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135031 #edb/ref 1065 3602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135031 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cf55fe35-583a-57a7-9a58-529f60897c64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135031 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135032 #edb/ref 1064 "A **unit vector** is a vector whose length is equal to $1.$ If we divide a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{x}$ by its norm $\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert,$ we obtain a unit vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\Vert\\mathbf{x}\\Vert},$ since\n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert\t=\\left\\Vert \\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert} \\right\\Vert\n\t\t\t\t\t\t= \\dfrac{1}{\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert}\\cdot\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n\t\t\t\t\t\t= 1.\n$$\n\nThe process of dividing a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ by its norm $\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert$ is called **normalizing the vector $\\mathbf{x}.$** We say that the unit vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\Vert\\mathbf{x}\\Vert}$ is *in the same direction* as $\\mathbf{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135032 #edb/ref 1065 3603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135032 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "65cfbc34-e842-5e88-aaa8-f40b697f1b1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135032 #edb/ref 1067 "Normalizing a Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135073 #edb/ref 1064 "In a Euclidean space, the **distance** between the vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the norm of the difference $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{y},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\text{d}(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{y} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nTo create a formula for the distance, we write the vectors in component form:\n![](images/01/011499b685af9a4c78d195755dc34e7b0d6ea0292eb2e9feafb94312fc7ee4b3.svg) \n\nNow, we can express the norm in terms of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}+⋯+({x}_{n}-{y}_{n})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can use the formula to find the distance between the vectors ![](images/2e/2e5d7cbac29024ca7f343363598714ea17bb180036d2f50f094a25584be2e23e.svg) and ![](images/37/37364e71c36f25701aa2e3b0ad368d4567b5719aa0c884ff0406ee9686125808.svg) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-(-5))^{2}+(4-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135073 #edb/ref 1065 3923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135073 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "00353d62-b024-52a9-a1ad-744f7f9e1492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135073 #edb/ref 1067 "The Distance Between Two Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135074 #edb/ref 1064 "We can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\Vert k \\mathbf{x} \\Vert= \\vert k \\vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector ![](images/43/430769431d512b87b6e67712fd8bd8de3802688f883600602a0d45b2749089cc.svg) To compute the norm of a multiple of $\\mathbf{x},$ say ${\\color{blue}-2}\\mathbf{x},$ we can factor the absolute value $|{\\color{blue}-2}|$ out of the norm as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =|-2|\\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can verify that we'd get the same result if we multiplied the $\\color{blue}-2$ first, before computing the norm. Multiplying by ${\\color{blue}-2},$ we have\n\n![](images/5d/5da07173bda8f167d9a0609457fd10f1e3fdcdd2de498fd08c1c0035a205e5e1.svg)\n\nThen, computing the norm, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135074 #edb/ref 1065 3924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135074 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "128f0f2d-0adc-5542-b2a2-20d7a7ed6223" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135074 #edb/ref 1067 "Factoring a Constant Out of the Norm" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269263 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269263 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269263 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "008faea9-9932-5ea0-a1b6-3854897c7f6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270611 #edb/ref 1080 "5\\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270611 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270611 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "987004f7-8842-5a33-a733-c34bc6057244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270672 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\sqrt{7}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270672 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270672 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9e509abf-c5d7-596f-8481-e8acfb0b83e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270939 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270939 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270939 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bd7a4161-0611-524c-bc9a-a0cf7707a898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271414 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271414 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271414 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f4c75e10-03eb-5923-a677-d6d077b71c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271569 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271569 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271569 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271569 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271569 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271569 #edb/ref 1